  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA surface cold front continues to progress south through the\nforecast area this morning, currently moving into the higher\nterrain. Isolated showers w/brief heavy downpours are possible\nacross the central/northern mountains as it crosses, with dry\nconditions then progged to return area-wide for the day on \nMonday. Some valley fog has developed across the southern\ncoalfields this morning in advance of the front, but do not \nanticipate much development further northward in the post- \nfrontal environment where increased mixing and upslope stratocu \ndevelopment should greatly inhibit fog development. \n\nTraversing surface high pressure to the north will bring a very\npleasant day to the area. Stratocu should quickly erode into a \nfair weather Cu field by late morning, with plenty of sunshine \nexpected for the afternoon/evening. Northwest flow aloft and \nassociated CAA will bring a seasonably cool day to the area, \nwith 70s expected across the lowlands. This transitions to a \ncool overnight, with lows progged in the 50s across the \nlowlands, while 40s in the mountains. The typical lowland valley\ncold spots could dip into the upper 40s, with upper 30s not out\nof the question in the coldest of the northern mountain \nvalleys. Return downsloping low-level flow will limit fog \nformation overnight, but given a significant temperature \ndifference between the rivers and air tonight, did insert patchy\nfog along the main stem rivers.\n\nHigh pressure continues to quickly shift further east of the \narea on Tuesday, with return flow and building ridging aloft \nresulting in a significant warmup for Tuesday afternoon onward. \nHighs on Tuesday will reach the mid 80s to 90 across the \nlowlands, with upper 80s to low 90s progged for Wednesday and \nThursday. Heat indices on Wednesday/Thursday could reach into \nthe mid/upper 90s as humidity continues to increase courtesy of \nsouthwest low-level flow. \n\nDry weather will persist area-wide on Tuesday. While much of the\narea remains dry on Wednesday, some modest diurnal buoyancy\ncould result in isolated shower/storm chances, primarily across \nthe higher terrain w/land forced ascent, as well as across our \nnorthern zones with the approach of a cold front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned frontal boundary is progged to then stall \n(east/west) and/or meander across the general Ohio Valley region\nWednesday night into the weekend, resulting in a somewhat more \nunsettled pattern with daily chances for showers/storms, \ngenerally favored in the afternoon/evening hours or when weak\nupper waves traverse overhead across the northern periphery of \nthe upper ridge. While confidence in specific details remains \nlow at this time, at least some hydro concerns could materialize\nunder the aforementioned pattern, with the threat for organized\nsevere weather being low given favorable kinematic fields being\ngenerally displaced too far to the north. High temperatures \nFriday into the weekend will continue to be above normal, albeit\nslightly cooler than Wednesday and Thursday.",
